Belschnickel's actually going to put your gift in the bowl once he's determined that you're admirable.

1797
01:14:12.140 --> 01:14:16.380
Only if you're admirable do you get a gift in the bowl, but the bowl is not your gift.

1798
01:14:16.500 --> 01:14:16.640
No.

1799
01:14:16.640 --> 01:14:17.560
He needs them all back.

1800
01:14:17.560 --> 01:14:18.300
They're a set.

1801
01:14:18.500 --> 01:14:18.780
Mm-hmm.

1802
01:14:18.780 --> 01:14:22.020
And if you're impish, you're going to get hit with those sticks.

1803
01:14:22.560 --> 01:14:22.900
Yes.

1804
01:14:23.200 --> 01:14:25.600
I have an unpopular opinion to share.

1805
01:14:25.820 --> 01:14:26.860
Oh, let's hear it.

1806
01:14:26.940 --> 01:14:27.940
Maybe it's unpopular.

1807
01:14:27.940 --> 01:14:28.960
Maybe it's popular.

1808
01:14:28.960 --> 01:14:29.580
We'll see.

1809
01:14:30.300 --> 01:14:31.000
Oh, my God.

1810
01:14:31.000 --> 01:14:32.360
I don't even know if I can say it.

1811
01:14:32.360 --> 01:14:33.960
I don't want people to turn on me.

1812
01:14:34.200 --> 01:14:35.800
Oh, is this like a Star Wars moment?

1813
01:14:36.160 --> 01:14:36.420
No.

1814
01:14:39.080 --> 01:14:51.380
It's just, you know, I think people are so hard on Pam in this season, but I don't love Jim in this episode.

1815
01:14:51.380 --> 01:14:52.280
Oh.

1816
01:14:52.540 --> 01:14:57.240
And I don't love this storyline for Jim and Pam in this episode.

1817
01:14:57.380 --> 01:14:59.600
I remember struggling with it a little bit at the time.

1818
01:14:59.840 --> 01:15:04.240
I don't like how Jim is heckling Dwight in this moment.

1819
01:15:05.060 --> 01:15:13.740
I don't like that they've set up this whole dream of Dwight's to come true just so that they can kind of like just…

1820
01:15:13.740 --> 01:15:14.320
Poke fun at it.

1821
01:15:14.380 --> 01:15:14.560
Yeah.

1822
01:15:14.680 --> 01:15:15.620
It's kind of mean.

1823
01:15:15.740 --> 01:15:16.200
I think it's mean.

1824
01:15:21.380 --> 01:15:23.720
They're being jerks, but I'm sorry.

1825
01:15:23.880 --> 01:15:42.200
I think in particular, Jim, and it was hard for me as Pam, who has a soft spot for Dwight, to find a way to do this thing for her husband that was resulting in her friend…

1826
01:15:42.200 --> 01:15:43.400
Getting made fun of.

1827
01:15:43.640 --> 01:15:44.040
Yeah.

1828
01:15:44.360 --> 01:15:46.280
And I just, I didn't like the snickering.

1829
01:15:46.280 --> 01:15:56.520
I didn't like what I also sort of saw as kind of this like smug, too cool for school kind of vibe.

1830
01:15:56.780 --> 01:15:59.080
That was coming from Jim.

1831
01:16:00.440 --> 01:16:00.920
Sorry.

1832
01:16:01.080 --> 01:16:02.300
I can see all that.

1833
01:16:02.460 --> 01:16:03.100
I can see it.

1834
01:16:03.300 --> 01:16:03.620
Yeah.

1835
01:16:03.840 --> 01:16:04.360
It was hard.

1836
01:16:04.460 --> 01:16:05.640
It was, I didn't like it.

1837
01:16:05.880 --> 01:16:09.440
I didn't find Jim likable in this storyline.

1838
01:16:09.940 --> 01:16:13.140
Also, just like, why haven't you said anything to Daryl?

1839
01:16:13.800 --> 01:16:14.280
Right?

1840
01:16:14.280 --> 01:16:16.500
Like, Jim's very self-involved.

1841
01:16:16.960 --> 01:16:18.320
Very self-involved.

1842
01:16:18.900 --> 01:16:32.060
He's having that moment where he has this new thing that is really distracting him and occupying all of his space and also maybe making him lose sight on the people that matter around him.

1843
01:16:32.400 --> 01:16:34.960
He's just having his little selfish chapter here.

1844
01:16:35.120 --> 01:16:35.740
He is.

1845
01:16:36.060 --> 01:16:38.220
And we all have them in our lives.

1846
01:16:38.220 --> 01:16:38.400
We do.

1847
01:16:38.400 --> 01:16:38.720
We do.

1848
01:16:45.200 --> 01:16:50.220
And then we come out of the fog and we're like, oh, I was kind of a turd back then.

1849
01:16:50.360 --> 01:16:50.780
I'm sorry.

1850
01:16:51.020 --> 01:16:51.300
Yes.

1851
01:16:51.420 --> 01:16:54.320
And that's okay because he'll come out of the fog.

1852
01:16:54.480 --> 01:16:55.700
You got to come out of the fog.

1853
01:16:55.820 --> 01:16:56.140
Yes.

1854
01:16:56.580 --> 01:16:57.640
Stop being a turd.

1855
01:16:58.100 --> 01:16:58.460
Yes.

1856
01:16:58.800 --> 01:17:02.480
Well, Jim is going to realize now, oh, my gosh, I have to go.

1857
01:17:02.640 --> 01:17:03.640
I have to go to Philly.

1858
01:17:04.180 --> 01:17:07.260
And both Pam and Dwight are taken aback.

1859
01:17:07.340 --> 01:17:08.500
They're like, oh, my gosh, already?

1860
01:17:08.720 --> 01:17:08.940
What?

1861
01:17:08.940 --> 01:17:11.400
They're both bummed out in their own different ways.

1862
01:17:11.920 --> 01:17:17.560
But before he leaves, Belschnickel needs to judge if he was impish or admirable.

1863
01:17:17.740 --> 01:17:18.760
And guess what he was?

1864
01:17:19.040 --> 01:17:20.640
Jim was impish.

1865
01:17:20.800 --> 01:17:21.680
So impish.

1866
01:17:21.700 --> 01:17:22.480
So impish.

1867
01:17:22.680 --> 01:17:26.520
We got a fan question from Ty B in Calgary, Alberta.

1868
01:17:26.800 --> 01:17:30.700
When Dwight was whacking Jim with the stick, is he actually hitting him?

1869
01:17:30.700 --> 01:17:32.220
It looks so real.

1870
01:17:33.000 --> 01:17:34.540
Uh, yeah.

1871
01:17:35.100 --> 01:17:35.680
Yeah.

1872
01:17:35.960 --> 01:17:37.400
I really hit him.

1873
01:17:37.400 --> 01:17:43.380
I remember the pieces of the stick breaking off as Rain whacked him with that.

1874
01:17:43.580 --> 01:17:44.280
I did, too.

1875
01:17:44.460 --> 01:17:48.340
When I watched the scene, I was like, oh, yeah, remember how hard he was hitting him?

1876
01:17:48.480 --> 01:17:53.360
I will tell you, there's a moment, and you can see, it's truly honest, where John is like,

1877
01:17:53.440 --> 01:17:54.080
okay, enough.

1878
01:17:54.280 --> 01:17:56.100
Yes, I saw it, too.

1879
01:17:56.660 --> 01:18:00.700
Well, we asked Rain if he remembered this scene, and here is what he said.

1880
01:18:01.320 --> 01:18:09.340
You know, the number one thing that I remember is, like, I was infamous for hurting people on the set.

1881
01:18:09.620 --> 01:18:18.060
At one point, I had thrown a mini barbell up in the air, and it hit, right, Robinson in the head and gave him a bump the size of a golf ball.

1882
01:18:18.460 --> 01:18:21.860
You know, I'd thrown a snowball in John's face.

1883
01:18:21.860 --> 01:18:25.640
I'd knocked sand into Leslie's eyes.

1884
01:18:26.000 --> 01:18:34.280
Like, everyone had some way, shape, or form had been hurt by Rain playing Dwight in some way, shape, or form.

1885
01:18:35.220 --> 01:18:42.320
So, I knew, like, oh, jeez, I've got to, like, literally whip people with a straw baton.

1886
01:18:43.180 --> 01:18:45.080
Someone's going to get their eye gouged out.

1887
01:18:46.380 --> 01:18:53.860
So, probably I hit John too many times and too hard, and I think a couple of times I tried to hit him in the groinal area,

1888
01:18:53.860 --> 01:18:56.580
just on purpose, just because it was so much fun.

1889
01:18:56.820 --> 01:19:01.360
I would like to just get a little reaction out of him.

1890
01:19:01.360 --> 01:19:05.640
So, I was very wary and careful around that.

1891
01:19:06.880 --> 01:19:09.400
Well, Rain, I remember all those same things.

1892
01:19:09.640 --> 01:19:10.240
I do, too.

1893
01:19:10.340 --> 01:19:15.620
I like how you said, Rain, that you were very careful around that area, but you still tried to hit it.

1894
01:19:15.720 --> 01:19:16.500
Yes, that's right.

1895
01:19:20.410 --> 01:19:30.630
Well, lady, before we move on, my neighbor is such a big fan of the show and wanted to make a message for us.

1896
01:19:30.750 --> 01:19:31.630
I hope that's okay.

1897
01:19:32.150 --> 01:19:32.570
Oh.

1898
01:19:32.570 --> 01:19:35.310
He's such a huge fan, and we'll hear.

1899
01:19:35.470 --> 01:19:36.830
You have to hear it.

1900
01:19:37.790 --> 01:19:39.110
Hey, Jenna.

1901
01:19:39.550 --> 01:19:40.550
Hi, Angela.

1902
01:19:40.850 --> 01:19:43.230
Happy 200th episode.

1903
01:19:43.630 --> 01:19:45.550
What a fantastic milestone.

1904
01:19:46.050 --> 01:19:49.630
Well, cool beans, and congratulations, girls.

1905
01:19:50.850 --> 01:19:52.110
Oh, my gosh.

1906
01:19:52.370 --> 01:19:55.310
I did not know what to expect when you set that up.

1907
01:19:55.530 --> 01:19:55.830
I know.

1908
01:19:55.950 --> 01:19:57.610
Your face, you were like, what?

1909
01:19:57.850 --> 01:20:02.990
Because I know I saw you doing that thing in your brain of like, who, what, who, what neighbor?

1910
01:20:03.310 --> 01:20:09.190
I thought it was going to be like a kid or somebody who lives in your neighborhood, and it was the biggest kid of all, Creed.

1911
01:20:09.450 --> 01:20:11.570
Creed, who lives at the end of my street.

1912
01:20:11.750 --> 01:20:11.970
Aw.

1913
01:20:11.970 --> 01:20:16.550
He's been so supportive of the podcast, and he wanted to wish us happy 200th.

1914
01:20:16.910 --> 01:20:17.930
I love that.

1915
01:20:18.530 --> 01:20:21.030
Well, now Pam is going to walk Jim to his cab.

1916
01:20:21.690 --> 01:20:22.890
She's like, this is it.

1917
01:20:23.050 --> 01:20:25.350
I can't believe it's actually happening.

1918
01:20:26.470 --> 01:20:30.890
And Jim says, thanks for putting together such a great last Christmas party.

1919
01:20:31.090 --> 01:20:32.770
I felt that on two levels.

1920
01:20:32.970 --> 01:20:34.170
I was like, this is it.

1921
01:20:34.350 --> 01:20:36.130
This is the last Christmas party.

1922
01:20:36.790 --> 01:20:40.630
Yeah, he also makes a joke, and he's like, well, I guess this is goodbye.

1923
01:20:40.910 --> 01:20:42.950
You know, I'll never see you again or something like that.

1924
01:20:43.050 --> 01:20:44.310
I can't remember the exact joke.

1925
01:20:44.410 --> 01:20:46.510
And Pam's like, come on, I'm trying to be serious here.

1926
01:20:46.850 --> 01:20:47.170
Yeah, yeah.

1927
01:20:47.330 --> 01:20:54.830
And I felt like this was another moment that we were trying to play to show how Jim's head is a little bit in the clouds.

1928
01:20:54.830 --> 01:20:56.550
He's enamored with the new job.

1929
01:20:56.690 --> 01:21:00.530
He's having that selfish time, and he's not seeing Pam.

1930
01:21:01.090 --> 01:21:05.930
He's not registering last Christmas party and, like, the look on her face.

1931
01:21:06.050 --> 01:21:11.230
He's not registering, like, the separation of their family so that this job can happen.

1932
01:21:11.490 --> 01:21:17.390
Yes, and also, I don't know if you noticed, but Pam says, I love you, as he's getting into the cab.

1933
01:21:17.510 --> 01:21:18.690
And he's like, oh, yeah, I love you, too.

1934
01:21:18.790 --> 01:21:22.230
It's like everything is a real afterthought for Jim here.

1935
01:21:22.230 --> 01:21:28.870
And it's meant to be kind of to show, like, oh, wow, they are in different spaces right now.

1936
01:21:29.290 --> 01:21:35.510
Well, Pam is going to very sadly walk back into the office, and Dwight has been watching all of this from the window.

1937
01:21:35.850 --> 01:21:40.630
Well, as Pam's going up to the office, Dwight is on his way out.

1938
01:21:40.890 --> 01:21:43.330
He's taken all of his decorations down.

1939
01:21:43.470 --> 01:21:44.350
He's like, you know what?

1940
01:21:44.490 --> 01:21:46.590
You bell on Christmas, Christmas bells on you.

1941
01:21:46.810 --> 01:21:47.870
And guess what?

1942
01:21:48.030 --> 01:21:49.250
Bell schniggle isn't real.

1943
01:21:49.450 --> 01:21:51.110
It's me, Dwight.

1944
01:21:51.110 --> 01:21:52.890
But yeah, he's hurt.

1945
01:21:53.030 --> 01:21:53.710
He is.

1946
01:21:54.030 --> 01:21:59.750
But the Christmas party is going to continue because Phyllis finds some old decorations in the warehouse.

1947
01:22:00.330 --> 01:22:02.770
Oscar ran to the store for food and drinks.

1948
01:22:02.970 --> 01:22:04.310
And Phyllis says, guess what?

1949
01:22:04.370 --> 01:22:05.870
I guess they needed me after all.

1950
01:22:06.130 --> 01:22:06.490
Mm-hmm.

1951
01:22:06.890 --> 01:22:09.950
And, Lady, I have to share with you, I have a little call sheet tidbit here.

1952
01:22:10.170 --> 01:22:12.530
I think I know what this call sheet tidbit is.

1953
01:22:12.710 --> 01:22:13.190
Yes.

1954
01:22:13.650 --> 01:22:18.750
The Dunder Mifflin crew is decorating for Christmas, but in real life on our set, it was Halloween.

1955
01:22:19.250 --> 01:22:20.570
Halloween was this week.

1956
01:22:20.570 --> 01:22:22.170
This used to happen all the time.

1957
01:22:22.170 --> 01:22:26.490
When we would shoot our Christmas episode, it would be Halloween week in real life.

1958
01:22:26.650 --> 01:22:28.070
This has happened more than once.

1959
01:22:28.650 --> 01:22:32.390
And boy, did our cast and crew love Halloween.

1960
01:22:32.990 --> 01:22:37.610
I mean, I shared in last week's episode, there was a countdown on the call sheets to Halloween.

1961
01:22:38.370 --> 01:22:41.770
Well, Steve Burgess sent me the call sheet for October 31St.

1962
01:22:41.830 --> 01:22:42.530
We were filming.

1963
01:22:42.850 --> 01:22:43.890
I printed it out.

1964
01:22:43.990 --> 01:22:44.750
I'm going to share it.

1965
01:22:45.050 --> 01:22:47.530
Someone was having a lot of fun with this call sheet, lady.

1966
01:22:47.530 --> 01:22:54.450
First of all, normally on a call sheet, it tells you the crew call and what time everyone's called to set.

1967
01:22:54.630 --> 01:22:58.930
On this one, it says the bewitching hour and the spooky call.

1968
01:23:00.090 --> 01:23:06.790
And then, normally we have a message if there's going to be an on-set photographer, and it will say, NBC still photographer on set today.

1969
01:23:07.170 --> 01:23:12.410
But on this call sheet announcement, it said the NBC still coroner will be on set today.

1970
01:23:12.410 --> 01:23:16.930
There's also announcement that the Halloween costume contest will be at lunch.

1971
01:23:17.090 --> 01:23:25.190
And then, if you go to the back of the call sheet, for all of the different crew categories, they were changed to different names.

1972
01:23:25.230 --> 01:23:30.190
For example, instead of saying hair and makeup with everyone's call times, it says morticians.

1973
01:23:31.770 --> 01:23:42.270
There's the headhunters, there's the groundskeepers, there's the bloody heart department, the grave robbers, the flamethrowers, the office of the recently deceased.

1974
01:23:42.810 --> 01:23:43.370
Amazing.

1975
01:23:43.610 --> 01:23:44.050
Amazing.

1976
01:23:44.690 --> 01:23:50.490
And in the transportation department, they were labeled, instead of transportation, hearses.

1977
01:23:50.810 --> 01:23:55.110
I mean, someone was just having a really good time with all of this, and I'm going to share it.

1978
01:23:55.170 --> 01:23:55.830
It's really fun.

1979
01:23:55.830 --> 01:24:03.770
Well, I remember this Halloween, because my son was a year old, and we were going to dress him up as a Wimbledon tennis player.

1980
01:24:03.890 --> 01:24:04.610
Yeah, I remember.

1981
01:24:04.830 --> 01:24:11.710
We had gone to Wimbledon when I was pregnant, and we had gotten a teeny baby-sized Wimbledon shirt.

1982
01:24:11.710 --> 01:24:12.490
It was white.

1983
01:24:12.670 --> 01:24:18.350
And we were like, oh, this is such a good costume, because he can wear the shirt, he can wear the little white shorts.

1984
01:24:18.910 --> 01:24:19.030
Right?

1985
01:24:19.490 --> 01:24:21.830
And little hat, little shoes.

1986
01:24:22.510 --> 01:24:27.410
And I was like, oh, I want to get him, like, a little tennis racket, like a little toy tennis racket.

1987
01:24:27.810 --> 01:24:31.750
Well, I couldn't find any that were, like, the right size for him.

1988
01:24:31.930 --> 01:24:33.050
Sure, they're all so big.

1989
01:24:33.250 --> 01:24:33.550
Yeah.

1990
01:24:33.930 --> 01:24:44.490
So I was just chatting with Phil Shea, the amazing Phil Shea, and I said, Phil, what would you do if you had to get, like, a tiny tennis racket for, like, a one-year-old?

1991
01:24:44.590 --> 01:24:45.770
Like, where would you get that?

1992
01:24:45.910 --> 01:24:46.170
Sure.

1993
01:24:46.670 --> 01:24:48.910
Where can you point me in the right direction?

1994
01:24:49.470 --> 01:24:51.530
And he said, Jenna, I got it.

1995
01:24:51.610 --> 01:24:52.150
I'll get you one.

1996
01:24:52.330 --> 01:24:53.050
I'll get you one.

1997
01:24:53.130 --> 01:24:53.830
I'm like, no, no, no.

1998
01:24:54.010 --> 01:24:55.270
Just, I'll do it.

1999
01:24:55.350 --> 01:24:56.870
Like, you don't need anything more to do.

2000
01:24:57.150 --> 01:24:58.310
He was like, no, I'll get it.

2001
01:24:58.370 --> 01:24:58.910
I'll get it for you.

2002
01:24:58.970 --> 01:24:59.370
I got it.

2003
01:24:59.470 --> 01:24:59.890
I got it.

2004
01:24:59.970 --> 01:25:00.670
I was like, okay.

2005
01:25:00.970 --> 01:25:02.770
This was like a few weeks before Halloween.

2006
01:25:03.350 --> 01:25:09.150
For Halloween, Angela, Phil Shea presented me with a tiny tennis racket lady.

2007
01:25:09.650 --> 01:25:10.870
It was not a toy.

2008
01:25:16.170 --> 01:25:23.690
He had someone make your son a real teeny tiny real racket with strings and everything.

2009
01:25:24.090 --> 01:25:27.910
Yes, with a teeny tiny tennis bag.

2010
01:25:28.110 --> 01:25:28.650
Oh, my gosh.

2011
01:25:28.730 --> 01:25:29.730
It was the cutest thing.

2012
01:25:29.910 --> 01:25:33.370
I was like, Phil, what is this?

2013
01:25:33.670 --> 01:25:35.550
I was like, what have you done?

2014
01:25:35.750 --> 01:25:37.030
And he was just smiling.

2015
01:25:37.270 --> 01:25:38.250
He was so delighted.

2016
01:25:38.570 --> 01:25:39.910
I have all these pictures.

2017
01:25:40.090 --> 01:25:41.970
I mean, this tennis racket made the outfit.

2018
01:25:41.970 --> 01:25:45.030
It was the sweetest thing.

2019
01:25:45.210 --> 01:25:50.390
And you know, guys, we all gathered recently for a celebration of life for Phil.

2020
01:25:51.190 --> 01:25:57.410
All of our crew came and celebrated him, the crews of other shows he had worked on.

2021
01:25:57.790 --> 01:26:03.270
It was so wonderful to see how many lives Phil touched.

2022
01:26:03.270 --> 01:26:12.090
It was so bittersweet, but also so special to see our crew and to spend time with them and to share our memories of Phil.

2023
01:26:12.330 --> 01:26:12.610
Yeah.

2024
01:26:12.750 --> 01:26:17.450
And this was a story that I told because it's so very Phil.

2025
01:26:17.730 --> 01:26:18.090
Yeah.

2026
01:26:18.710 --> 01:26:19.270
Yeah.

2027
01:26:19.430 --> 01:26:21.230
So I remember this, and I have pictures.

2028
01:26:21.550 --> 01:26:22.410
I do, too.

2029
01:26:22.610 --> 01:26:24.690
I love the pictures from this Halloween.

2030
01:26:24.690 --> 01:26:29.350
And also, Steve Burgess shared some of his photos from the Halloween costume conteSt.

2031
01:26:29.350 --> 01:26:35.570
Our crew would often coordinate costumes, and so there was a group this year, I remember, who did Scooby-Doo.

2032
01:26:35.750 --> 01:26:37.250
They were so creative.

2033
01:26:37.870 --> 01:26:43.250
I have a photo that I love of my daughter and your son on set together.

2034
01:26:43.430 --> 01:26:50.470
He's in his tennis outfit, and you would never know this because she's just in a pink shirt and pink leggings, but she was a cupcake,

2035
01:26:50.850 --> 01:26:53.750
like a papier-mâché cupcake that you step into.

2036
01:26:54.130 --> 01:26:57.890
But she got tired of wearing it, so she took it off.

2037
01:26:57.890 --> 01:26:59.410
She was just the underneath part.

2038
01:26:59.650 --> 01:27:02.210
Yeah, but they were a cupcake and a tennis player.

2039
01:27:02.510 --> 01:27:04.130
This was a really special episode.

2040
01:27:04.350 --> 01:27:05.690
Everyone brought their kids.

2041
01:27:05.870 --> 01:27:06.830
It was really special.

2042
01:27:07.110 --> 01:27:11.410
It was our last Christmas episode and our last Halloween parade all in one.

2043
01:27:11.610 --> 01:27:11.830
Yep.

2044
01:27:12.270 --> 01:27:17.250
Nellie hears the party starting, but Toby is still sharing with her about his jury experience.

2045
01:27:17.510 --> 01:27:22.130
There's a great montage in the bullpen of people starting to have a good time.

2046
01:27:22.130 --> 01:27:28.650
At 16 minutes, 33 seconds, did anyone else catch Brian as Kevin lifting me off the credenza?

2047
01:27:28.850 --> 01:27:29.110
Yes!

2048
01:27:29.190 --> 01:27:30.410
And almost dropping me?

2049
01:27:30.490 --> 01:27:31.310
What was that?

2050
01:27:31.530 --> 01:27:33.430
We just improvised that.

2051
01:27:33.610 --> 01:27:39.030
I was supposed to be up there hanging decorations, and I legitimately couldn't get down and needed help.

2052
01:27:39.230 --> 01:27:43.110
And Brian thought it would be funny to kind of clumsily get me down from there.

2053
01:27:43.330 --> 01:27:45.930
It looks like also Kevin got his wish.

2054
01:27:46.090 --> 01:27:46.730
He is Santa.

2055
01:27:46.730 --> 01:27:46.870
Santa.

2056
01:27:47.010 --> 01:27:47.630
That's right.

2057
01:27:48.350 --> 01:27:52.690
Daryl is really hitting that punch bowl hard and getting more and more drunk.

2058
01:27:52.990 --> 01:27:54.190
Dwight is going to return.

2059
01:27:54.190 --> 01:27:55.450
He's in his own clothes.

2060
01:27:55.590 --> 01:27:58.550
And Pam says, you know, for the record, I liked your party better.

2061
01:27:59.190 --> 01:28:03.430
Yeah, they're kind of commiserating, but they're going to go ahead and, you know,

2062
01:28:03.590 --> 01:28:07.710
participate in the party when all of a sudden, surprise, surprise, who walks in the door?

2063
01:28:08.350 --> 01:28:08.830
Jim.

2064
01:28:09.170 --> 01:28:09.650
Jim!

2065
01:28:10.210 --> 01:28:10.690
Yeah.

2066
01:28:11.090 --> 01:28:11.970
Jim is back.

2067
01:28:12.430 --> 01:28:13.570
What's going on?

2068
01:28:14.330 --> 01:28:15.290
Turns out...

2069
01:28:15.290 --> 01:28:16.290
He missed his wife.

2070
01:28:16.730 --> 01:28:16.990
Yeah.

2071
01:28:17.370 --> 01:28:19.530
And he also found out there's a bus at 5 a.m.

2072
01:28:19.710 --> 01:28:21.250
Why is he taking the bus?

2073
01:28:21.530 --> 01:28:22.170
I don't know.

2074
01:28:22.270 --> 01:28:23.450
They have two cars.

2075
01:28:24.270 --> 01:28:25.010
I don't know.

2076
01:28:25.010 --> 01:28:26.530
Does he not need a car when he's there?

2077
01:28:26.530 --> 01:28:28.050
Maybe he doesn't have a parking spot.

2078
01:28:28.630 --> 01:28:30.150
Is it hard to park in Philly?

2079
01:28:30.330 --> 01:28:33.870
I mean, he could just get there faster in his own car.

2080
01:28:33.990 --> 01:28:34.690
I don't know.

2081
01:28:34.890 --> 01:28:36.170
And then he would have a car.

2082
01:28:36.410 --> 01:28:38.650
And guys, it's not that far away.

2083
01:28:38.790 --> 01:28:39.230
I know.

2084
01:28:39.430 --> 01:28:44.630
I mean, frankly, he could drive there in the morning, get his work day done, and drive home.

2085
01:28:44.650 --> 01:28:45.750
He could be a commuter.

2086
01:28:46.420 --> 01:28:46.670
Yes.

2087
01:28:46.670 --> 01:28:47.810
People do that all the time.

2088
01:28:48.190 --> 01:28:48.750
All the time.

2089
01:28:49.170 --> 01:28:51.070
Well, Dwight is so happy.

2090
01:28:51.230 --> 01:28:52.290
Pam is so happy.

2091
01:28:52.850 --> 01:28:56.510
Dwight wants to compete for the big piece of the pig rib with Jim.

2092
01:28:57.130 --> 01:29:00.470
Jim wins, and oh, gosh, this was disgusting.

2093
01:29:00.730 --> 01:29:01.770
What was it?

2094
01:29:01.930 --> 01:29:02.550
I don't know.

2095
01:29:02.550 --> 01:29:08.190
No, it was like the wishing bone was like covered in, I don't know, it looked like some kind

2096
01:29:08.190 --> 01:29:10.310
of brothy gravy.

2097
01:29:11.230 --> 01:29:12.670
And it looks gross.

2098
01:29:13.050 --> 01:29:15.650
It splatters all over everyone.

2099
01:29:15.950 --> 01:29:17.110
I wouldn't want that.

2100
01:29:17.590 --> 01:29:19.070
I wouldn't want that either.

2101
01:29:19.070 --> 01:29:22.250
I mean, you know, I don't want a pie in the face.

2102
01:29:22.250 --> 01:29:24.850
I don't want you to smush my wedding cake in my face.

2103
01:29:24.870 --> 01:29:25.770
No, don't do that to me.

2104
01:29:25.770 --> 01:29:27.250
I don't want you to throw gravy on me.

2105
01:29:27.430 --> 01:29:28.770
Please don't throw gravy on me.

2106
01:29:29.690 --> 01:29:32.930
Daryl sees Jim, and oh, my gosh, he is ticked off.

2107
01:29:33.030 --> 01:29:36.150
He's going to charge towards Jim and let him have it.

2108
01:29:36.250 --> 01:29:40.450
And Jim's like, hey, I talked to the guys about hiring you, and they want to set up an

2109
01:29:40.450 --> 01:29:40.770
interview.

2110
01:29:41.210 --> 01:29:42.010
Daryl's like, what?

2111
01:29:42.430 --> 01:29:43.370
Oh, my gosh, great.

2112
01:29:43.630 --> 01:29:49.570
He does this like, woo-boo, spin move, and completely crashes into the food table.

2113
01:29:49.890 --> 01:29:55.130
We had a fan question from Kelly Kay in St. Louis, Missouri, who said, I need to know every

2114
01:29:55.130 --> 01:29:59.430
detail about Daryl falling on the table because it's one of my favorite moments of the whole

2115
01:29:59.430 --> 01:29:59.910
series.

2116
01:30:00.290 --> 01:30:04.450
The spin before he falls, the table breaking twice, everyone screaming.

2117
01:30:04.450 --> 01:30:06.870
It just cracks me up every single time I watch.

2118
01:30:07.570 --> 01:30:11.290
Well, Steve Burgess said we had a special breakaway table built.

2119
01:30:11.290 --> 01:30:14.830
Our stunt coordinator, Brett Jones, worked out the fall with Craig.

2120
01:30:15.090 --> 01:30:16.270
Craig really did it himself.

2121
01:30:16.270 --> 01:30:17.710
That was not a stunt person.

2122
01:30:17.710 --> 01:30:18.670
I think you can tell.

2123
01:30:19.610 --> 01:30:22.790
And Steve said we were prepared to do two takes.

2124
01:30:23.010 --> 01:30:28.130
We had two breakaway tables, but Craig nailed it on the first go, so we only had to do the

2125
01:30:28.130 --> 01:30:28.410
one.

2126
01:30:29.010 --> 01:30:32.310
Angela, we also had a fan catch in this scene from Anna W.

2127
01:30:32.510 --> 01:30:33.690
in Edmond, Oklahoma.

2128
01:30:34.030 --> 01:30:34.850
Edmond, Oklahoma.

2129
01:30:34.930 --> 01:30:36.090
I was just there.

2130
01:30:36.330 --> 01:30:36.970
You were?

2131
01:30:37.170 --> 01:30:38.770
Yeah, Josh's sister lives there.

2132
01:30:39.310 --> 01:30:41.270
Does she know Anna W.?

2133
01:30:41.290 --> 01:30:42.110
I don't know.

2134
01:30:42.370 --> 01:30:43.510
We'll have to find out.

2135
01:30:43.610 --> 01:30:44.290
What's up, Anna?

2136
01:30:44.710 --> 01:30:50.910
Well, Anna says between 19 and 20 minutes, we catch a glimpse of Phyllis at the party wearing

2137
01:30:50.910 --> 01:30:51.870
a fur coat.

2138
01:30:52.230 --> 01:30:56.050
Was there a side story that explains why she's wearing a fur coat?

2139
01:30:56.270 --> 01:30:58.030
Oh, Anna, I got you covered.

2140
01:30:58.510 --> 01:30:58.950
All right.

2141
01:30:58.950 --> 01:31:03.470
So there was a montage of the Christmas party where everyone was having a good time, right?

2142
01:31:03.550 --> 01:31:04.270
I mentioned that?

2143
01:31:04.490 --> 01:31:06.670
Well, it's longer in extended.

2144
01:31:06.870 --> 01:31:08.110
It's in deleted scenes.

2145
01:31:08.110 --> 01:31:11.350
And Bob Vance gives Phyllis her Christmas present.

2146
01:31:11.350 --> 01:31:13.330
Again, why at the work party?

2147
01:31:13.750 --> 01:31:15.250
This happens sometimes.

2148
01:31:15.570 --> 01:31:15.790
It does.

2149
01:31:15.790 --> 01:31:17.370
We get big gifts at these parties.

2150
01:31:17.450 --> 01:31:17.790
Yes.

2151
01:31:17.990 --> 01:31:19.370
And it's a fur coat.

2152
01:31:19.870 --> 01:31:22.510
And he wraps her in it, and then they snuggle.

2153
01:31:22.510 --> 01:31:23.570
She's so happy.

2154
01:31:24.170 --> 01:31:30.950
Also in this scene, Creed is walking around with a tray of hot chocolate and tapas.

2155
01:31:31.150 --> 01:31:32.810
Oh, I love that.

2156
01:31:33.050 --> 01:31:35.650
This is how the scene was described in the shooting draft.

2157
01:31:35.650 --> 01:31:40.610
The office is decorated in a slapdash fashion for a last-second Christmas party.

2158
01:31:40.850 --> 01:31:45.470
The table is laden with food and drink, a few strings of lights, Christmas music.

2159
01:31:45.690 --> 01:31:46.930
People are having fun.

2160
01:31:47.370 --> 01:31:50.890
Phyllis, Angela, and Oscar look around at their handiwork and are proud.

2161
01:31:51.150 --> 01:31:52.930
People start taking shots.

2162
01:31:53.450 --> 01:31:55.430
Creed hands out hot chocolate and tapas.

2163
01:31:55.670 --> 01:31:59.190
Bob Vance watches Phyllis open her gift, a luxurious mink coat.

2164
01:31:59.410 --> 01:32:00.830
It takes her breath away.

2165
